Loop Diuretic Toxicity (Frusemide Overuse)
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Renal Loss
Profound acute weight loss, Severe muscle cramps, Pre-renal AKI with hypokalemia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-027
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation for severe dizziness, incapacitating muscle spasms, and profound weakness following rapid fluid loss, indicating critical volume depletion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented severe orthostatic syncope resulting in a near-fall, alongside agonising calf cramps after the patient self-escalated their diuretic dose. This presentation necessitated an urgent after-hours medical evaluation to assess hemodynamic stability, prevent traumatic injury, and initiate oral electrolyte resuscitation prior to the next standard in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Terrible leg cramps, severe dizziness when standing, and intense thirst. Patient states, I took extra water pills for three days to get rid of my ankle swelling, but now I can't even stand up without the room going black, and my calves are locked in agony.
History of Present Illness: Over the past 72 hours, the patient autonomously doubled their prescribed Frusemide dose. This resulted in excessive, rapid diuresis leading to profound, acute weight loss of 4 kilograms. Yesterday, the patient developed severe, painful muscle cramps particularly affecting the lower extremities. Today, symptoms escalated to pronounced orthostatic hypotension producing severe dizziness and near-syncope upon attempting to ambulate. The patient reports extreme lethargy, generalized weakness, and profound fatigue. A subjective feeling of severe, unquenchable thirst is present. Palpitations have occurred intermittently, raising suspicion for cardiac arrhythmias secondary to severe electrolyte depletion.
Functional Status: Confined to the floor after sliding down due to dizziness; entirely unable to safely ambulate.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extremely high risk of syncope and subsequent traumatic fracture; patient is living alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/80 mmHg (Supine) -> 85/55 mmHg (Standing) | HR: 112 bpm | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 71.0 Kg (Baseline 75.0 Kg)
General: Appears clinically dehydrated, anxious, rubbing bilateral calves, and visibly weak.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia is marked, out of proportion to exertion or fever. There is a profound orthostatic drop in blood pressure. Decreased jugular venous pressure is visible, with neck veins completely flat even in the supine position. Respiratory: Clear bilaterally. Integumentary: Examination reveals decreased skin turgor (tenting) and exceptionally dry mucous membranes. Musculoskeletal: The diagnostic triad is clinically evident. Profound acute weight loss is confirmed by history and home scale records. Severe muscle cramps are actively palpable in the gastrocnemius muscles. Clinical signs highly suspicious for pre-renal AKI with hypokalemia are present, pending laboratory confirmation.
Environmental Observations: Home is cluttered; trip hazards are numerous, exacerbating the fall risk associated with orthostasis.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient confirmed taking 160mg Frusemide daily instead of the prescribed 40mg.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Loop Diuretic Toxicity (Frusemide Overuse) - Status: Acute, decompensated volume status
Differentials:

Acute Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, or fever; fluid loss is purely renal.

Diabetic Ketoacidosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal capillary blood glucose and absence of Kussmaul respirations or ketone odor, isolating the polyuria to medication effect.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Calf pain is distinctly described as bilateral cramping spasms responsive to stretching, lacking unilateral swelling, erythema, or constant deep aching.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe iatrogenic hypovolemia and suspected profound electrolyte derangement secondary to loop diuretic toxicity. The excessive forced diuresis has resulted in massive intravascular volume depletion, evidenced by critical orthostatic hypotension and significant acute weight loss. The severe muscle spasms and palpitations are highly indicative of consequential hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period carried an unacceptable risk of a severe syncopal fall resulting in fracture or a fatal hypokalemia-induced cardiac arrhythmia. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to immediately cease the offending medication, assess perfusion, institute strict fall precautions, and initiate immediate oral electrolyte replacement as a safe bridge to urgent morning laboratory investigation. The expected investigation results include significantly elevated blood urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio greater than 20:1 with concurrent severe hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia that would assist in confirming the diagnosis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Mandated immediate cessation of all Frusemide. Initiated aggressive oral rehydration using a commercial electrolyte replacement solution. Educated patient on strict two-stage standing techniques.
Positive Behavioral Change: To correct your severe dehydration and stop the cramping, commit to drinking 250ml of electrolyte solution every hour. Keep a written log on the kitchen counter. When you review the log and simultaneously notice your leg cramps easing and your dizziness fading, explicitly acknowledge that you are actively replenishing your critical salt and water levels. This physical relief will reinforce your adherence to the structured hydration schedule.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review first thing tomorrow morning for comprehensive serum electrolytes and renal function testing (UEC, Magnesium).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the nearest Emergency Department immediately or call 000 if you experience a complete loss of consciousness, if you develop severe, continuous chest palpitations, or if you become entirely unable to stand up despite resting and hydrating.

